-- title:
--   Thresholds, transitions and parent returns for candidates
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that every reflexive real Banach space has the fixed point property for nonexpansive maps: every $F:C\to C$ with $\|Fa-Fb\|\le\|a-b\|$ on a nonempty closed bounded convex set $C$ has a fixed point. The proof assumes a fixed-point-free map, reduces to a minimal invariant set $K$ of diameter $1$, builds an adaptive weighted tree of nonexpansive maps generated by resolvents, and contradicts the minimality through detecting functionals. The tree is built level by level from candidate records: each candidate is a generated map attached to a parent node together with a norm-one functional chosen after the map, and every candidate, including those that later receive coefficient zero, consumes a global ordinal $n$ with budget $2^{-(n+9)}$. Fix a candidate context with anchor $a$.
--
--   1. **States.** Chronologically legal histories of candidate records.
--   2. **Threshold.** For a parent $p$ and a state, an integer $m\ge1$, computed from the history alone, such that every map $g$ in the resolvent tail of order $m$ extends the history by a legal record $(p,g,\varphi)$.
--   3. **Transition.** Appending exactly one legal record whose map is the selected map.
--   4. **Parent return.** An adaptive return to the anchor: a finite run of legal transitions, with convex coefficients $\beta$ such that
--   $$
--   \Bigl\|\sum_i\beta_ig_i(a)-a\Bigr\|<\tau ,
--   $$
--   and the order embedding of the positively weighted candidates. It is produced from the resolvent anchor.
